{"id":11631636772084,"type":"song","name":"Deacon Blues","artist":"Steely Dan","year":1978,"weight":11,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/69cc1f771bc7a8cf79affdcf14bae380/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"Aja","yt":"ICK6e9WK2A8"},"artist_ref":{"id":167361086744098,"name":"Steely Dan","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Steely_Dan_-_Donald_Fagen_-_Luzern_2007.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":49580741702968,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1978","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1978,"rank":100,"year":1978,"note":"","total":100,"scaruffi":null},{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":100,"year":1978,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"A top 20 hit on the Billboard Hot 100 in 1978, this song portrays a midlife crisis through the story of a \"discontented supermanite\" who desires a grandiose nickname like \"Deacon Blues.\" Originally conceived with a big band arrangement, it was crafted with layered rhythm tracks, vocals, and horns, including Tom Scott on sax, aiming for a \"tight, romantic, and Duke Ellington Cloudfield\" backdrop. Walter Becker clarified that the song is about an \"LLL loser\" and a \"broken dream of a broken man living his broken life,\" not about achieving a dream. \"Deacon Blues, a top 20 hit on the Billboard Hot 100 in 1978.\"","year":1977,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-38-the-influence-of-jazz-on-70s-popular-music"},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":null}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":153556251847105,"type":"album","name":"Aja","year":1977,"weight":60},{"id":170898324776216,"type":"album","name":"Countdown to Ecstasy","ye